{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-134529" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the percentage error obtained when approximating the value of $(0.9)^{6}$ using the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{6}$ in ascending powers of $x.$ Round your final percentage error to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069538/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9f4e31ddb8e442c141436aadb0a801f16e81b476d3d5fd0f85d8eca20a04a26"} {"question" "q-134529" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{6} & =1+6x+\\frac{1}{2!}\\cdot 6\\cdot 5\\cdot {x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n} \\\\  & =1+6x+15{x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{6}≈1+6x+15{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=-0.1$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{6}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0.9)^{6} & =(1+(-0.1))^{6} \\\\  & ≈1+6(-0.1)+15(-0.1)^{2} \\\\  & =1-6(0.1)+15(0.01) \\\\  & =1-0.6+0.15 \\\\  & =0.55\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}%\\,error & =\\frac{\\text{amount of error}}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100 \\\\  & =\\frac{|(0.9)^{6}-0.55|}{(0.9)^{6}}\\times 100 \\\\  & ≈3.49%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069538/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9f4e31ddb8e442c141436aadb0a801f16e81b476d3d5fd0f85d8eca20a04a26"} {"question" "q-134529" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069538/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fc207a0f545f4bcddfc7fe2d7b525056f1b74f876bdec3a0f2fd4546357c0572"} {"question" "q-134529" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069538/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9f4e31ddb8e442c141436aadb0a801f16e81b476d3d5fd0f85d8eca20a04a26"} {"question" "q-134529" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3.45%"] ["math" "3.47%"] ["math" "3.49%"] ["math" "3.52%"] ["math" "3.55%"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069538/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9f4e31ddb8e442c141436aadb0a801f16e81b476d3d5fd0f85d8eca20a04a26"} {"question" "q-134529" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3.49%"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069538/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9f4e31ddb8e442c141436aadb0a801f16e81b476d3d5fd0f85d8eca20a04a26"} {"question" "q-134648" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(3+x)^{5}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(2.96)^{5}.$ Give your answer to three decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069538/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9f4e31ddb8e442c141436aadb0a801f16e81b476d3d5fd0f85d8eca20a04a26"} {"question" "q-134648" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=3,$ $b=x,$ and $n=5,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(3+x)}^{5} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0}){(3)}^{5}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}){(3)}^{5-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}){(3)}^{5-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(3)}^{5}+5{(3)}^{4}{(x)}^{1}+10{(3)}^{3}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =243+5(81)(x)+10(27)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =243+405x+270{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(3+x)}^{5}≈243+405x+270{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.04$ into the above approximation of $(3+x)^{5}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2.96)^{5} & =(3+(-0.04))^{5} \\\\  & ≈243+405(-0.04)+270(-0.04)^{2} \\\\  & =243-405(0.04)+270(0.001\\,6) \\\\  & =243-16.2+0.432 \\\\  & =227.232,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto three decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069538/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9f4e31ddb8e442c141436aadb0a801f16e81b476d3d5fd0f85d8eca20a04a26"} {"question" "q-134648" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069538/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fc207a0f545f4bcddfc7fe2d7b525056f1b74f876bdec3a0f2fd4546357c0572"} {"question" "q-134648" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069538/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9f4e31ddb8e442c141436aadb0a801f16e81b476d3d5fd0f85d8eca20a04a26"} {"question" "q-134648" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "227.226"] ["math" "227.228"] ["math" "227.230"] ["math" "227.232"] ["math" "227.234"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069538/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9f4e31ddb8e442c141436aadb0a801f16e81b476d3d5fd0f85d8eca20a04a26"} {"question" "q-134648" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "227.232"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069538/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9f4e31ddb8e442c141436aadb0a801f16e81b476d3d5fd0f85d8eca20a04a26"} {"question" "q-134649" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(4+x)^{5}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(3.89)^{5}.$ Give your answer to three decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069538/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9f4e31ddb8e442c141436aadb0a801f16e81b476d3d5fd0f85d8eca20a04a26"} {"question" "q-134649" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=4,$ $b=x,$ and $n=5,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(4+x)}^{5} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0}){(4)}^{5}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}){(4)}^{5-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}){(4)}^{5-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(4)}^{5}+5{(4)}^{4}{(x)}^{1}+10{(4)}^{3}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1\\,024+5(256)(x)+10(64)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =1\\,024+1\\,280x+640{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(4+x)}^{5}≈1\\,024+1\\,280x+640{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.11$ into the above approximation of $(4+x)^{5}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3.89)^{5} & =(4+(-0.11))^{5} \\\\  & ≈1\\,024+1\\,280(-0.11)+640(-0.11)^{2} \\\\  & =1\\,024-1\\,280(0.11)+640(0.012\\,1) \\\\  & =1\\,024-140.8+7.744 \\\\  & =890.944,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto three decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069538/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9f4e31ddb8e442c141436aadb0a801f16e81b476d3d5fd0f85d8eca20a04a26"} {"question" "q-134649" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069538/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fc207a0f545f4bcddfc7fe2d7b525056f1b74f876bdec3a0f2fd4546357c0572"} {"question" "q-134649" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069538/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9f4e31ddb8e442c141436aadb0a801f16e81b476d3d5fd0f85d8eca20a04a26"} {"question" "q-134649" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "890.734"] ["math" "890.825"] ["math" "890.892"] ["math" "890.944"] ["math" "890.967"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069538/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9f4e31ddb8e442c141436aadb0a801f16e81b476d3d5fd0f85d8eca20a04a26"} {"question" "q-134649" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "890.944"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069538/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9f4e31ddb8e442c141436aadb0a801f16e81b476d3d5fd0f85d8eca20a04a26"} {"question" "q-134653" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(2+x)^{8}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(1.87)^{8}.$ Give your answer to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069538/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9f4e31ddb8e442c141436aadb0a801f16e81b476d3d5fd0f85d8eca20a04a26"} {"question" "q-134653" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=2,$ $b=x,$ and $n=8,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(2+x)}^{8} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{0}){(2)}^{8}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{1}){(2)}^{8-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{2}){(2)}^{8-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(2)}^{8}+8{(2)}^{7}{(x)}^{1}+28{(2)}^{6}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =256+8(128)(x)+28(64)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =256+1\\,024x+1\\,792{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(2+x)}^{8}≈256+1\\,024x+1\\,792{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.13$ into the above approximation of $(2+x)^{8}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.87)^{8} & =(2+(-0.13))^{8} \\\\  & ≈256+1\\,024(-0.13)+1\\,792(-0.13)^{2} \\\\  & =256-1\\,024(0.13)+1\\,792(0.016\\,9) \\\\  & =256-133.12+30.284\\,8 \\\\  & =153.16,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069538/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9f4e31ddb8e442c141436aadb0a801f16e81b476d3d5fd0f85d8eca20a04a26"} {"question" "q-134653" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069538/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fc207a0f545f4bcddfc7fe2d7b525056f1b74f876bdec3a0f2fd4546357c0572"} {"question" "q-134653" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069538/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9f4e31ddb8e442c141436aadb0a801f16e81b476d3d5fd0f85d8eca20a04a26"} {"question" "q-134653" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "149.53"] ["math" "150.45"] ["math" "151.13"] ["math" "152.23"] ["math" "153.16"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069538/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9f4e31ddb8e442c141436aadb0a801f16e81b476d3d5fd0f85d8eca20a04a26"} {"question" "q-134653" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "153.16"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069538/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9f4e31ddb8e442c141436aadb0a801f16e81b476d3d5fd0f85d8eca20a04a26"} {"question" "q-178289" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{10}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(0.999)^{10}.$ Give your answer to six decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069538/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9f4e31ddb8e442c141436aadb0a801f16e81b476d3d5fd0f85d8eca20a04a26"} {"question" "q-178289" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{10} & =1+10x+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10\\cdot 9\\cdot {x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{10} \\\\  & =1+10x+45{x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{10}≈1+10x+45{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.001$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{10}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0.999)^{10} & =(1+(-0.001))^{10} \\\\  & ≈1+10(-0.001)+45(-0.001)^{2} \\\\  & =1-10(0.001)+45(0.000\\,001) \\\\  & =1-0.01+0.000\\,045 \\\\  & =0.990\\,045,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto six decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069538/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9f4e31ddb8e442c141436aadb0a801f16e81b476d3d5fd0f85d8eca20a04a26"} {"question" "q-178289" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069538/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fc207a0f545f4bcddfc7fe2d7b525056f1b74f876bdec3a0f2fd4546357c0572"} {"question" "q-178289" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069538/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9f4e31ddb8e442c141436aadb0a801f16e81b476d3d5fd0f85d8eca20a04a26"} {"question" "q-178289" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.009\\,955"] ["math" "0.990\\,043"] ["math" "0.990\\,045"] ["math" "0.999\\,950"] ["math" "1.010\\,045"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069538/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9f4e31ddb8e442c141436aadb0a801f16e81b476d3d5fd0f85d8eca20a04a26"} {"question" "q-178289" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.990\\,045"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069538/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9f4e31ddb8e442c141436aadb0a801f16e81b476d3d5fd0f85d8eca20a04a26"}] "usage" {} "basis" 2377 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
